<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                   xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
        Drop attachment.exercise_id, the last remnant of an attachment that belongs to an exercise.

        The column comes from the generated schema this project started from and has never had a writer: no released
        version has an endpoint that creates an attachment for an exercise, and no code path sets one. The mapping was
        there in name only. Exercise.attachments was a lazy inverse collection whose three touch points all *emptied*
        it (both import services and Exercise.disconnectRelatedEntities), the single reader was a ternary in
        GroupNotificationService that could only ever take its lecture branch, and ProgrammingExerciseRepository
        declared a fetch option for it that nothing selected.

        Nor is a row reachable if one somehow exists. Every attachment file route in FileResource is keyed by a lecture
        or by an attachment video unit, and FilePathType has no case for an exercise, so there is no URI that resolves
        such an attachment's file and no page that lists one. Dropping the column therefore removes a reference that
        pointed nowhere rather than losing access to anything.

        This is a plain drop rather than the contract half of an expand/contract: there is no writer on the previous
        version whose insert could fail while it is still serving during a rolling deployment. attachment.lecture_id is
        the opposite case and is deliberately NOT dropped here, see the note at the end of this file.

        The initial schema created an explicit index on the column that carries the foreign key's own generated name,
        so the constraint is dropped first and the index after it. Both databases keep that index once the constraint
        is gone, and both would drop it along with the column anyway, so the separate drop is there to be explicit
        rather than because the column drop needs it, and it is guarded by a precondition so that a database which
        never had the index under that name skips it instead of failing.
    -->
    <changeSet id="20260907100622-1-drop-attachment-exercise-foreign-key" author="krusche">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <foreignKeyConstraintExists foreignKeyTableName="attachment" foreignKeyName="FK840kdpn5r9l2wyhq83gl6sx2g"/>
        </preConditions>
        <comment>Drop the foreign key from attachment.exercise_id to exercise.</comment>
        <dropForeignKeyConstraint baseTableName="attachment" constraintName="FK840kdpn5r9l2wyhq83gl6sx2g"/>
        <rollback>
            <addForeignKeyConstraint baseColumnNames="exercise_id" baseTableName="attachment" constraintName="FK840kdpn5r9l2wyhq83gl6sx2g" deferrable="false"
                                     initiallyDeferred="false" onDelete="RESTRICT" onUpdate="RESTRICT" referencedColumnNames="id" referencedTableName="exercise"
                                     validate="true"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20260907100622-2-drop-attachment-exercise-index" author="krusche">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <indexExists tableName="attachment" indexName="FK840kdpn5r9l2wyhq83gl6sx2g"/>
        </preConditions>
        <comment>Drop the index on attachment.exercise_id where the database kept it after the foreign key was dropped.</comment>
        <dropIndex tableName="attachment" indexName="FK840kdpn5r9l2wyhq83gl6sx2g"/>
        <rollback>
            <createIndex indexName="FK840kdpn5r9l2wyhq83gl6sx2g" tableName="attachment">
                <column name="exercise_id"/>
            </createIndex>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20260907100622-3-drop-attachment-exercise-column" author="krusche">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <columnExists tableName="attachment" columnName="exercise_id"/>
        </preConditions>
        <comment>
            Drop the column itself. Dropping a column commits on its own on MySQL, so the precondition is what makes
            this changeset safe to run again after a start that was killed between the statement and the
            databasechangelog row.
        </comment>
        <dropColumn tableName="attachment" columnName="exercise_id"/>
        <rollback>
            <addColumn tableName="attachment">
                <column name="exercise_id" type="bigint"/>
            </addColumn>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
        Why attachment.lecture_id is not dropped here.

        Nothing maps it any longer: Attachment lost its lecture reference in this release, and the endpoint that serves
        a file still lying under uploads/attachments/lecture/{lectureId} now reaches the lecture through the attachment
        video unit that owns the attachment, selecting on the link prefix instead of on the column.

        Dropping it, though, is the contract half of an expand/contract and must wait for the release after this one.
        The previous version's LectureImportService copies lecture.attachments when a lecture is imported and writes
        lecture_id on each copy, so a node still serving during a rolling deployment would fail on that insert the
        moment the column is gone. The same import is also what can leave a straggler behind: a row with a lecture_id
        and no attachment_unit_id, which this version does not show and whose file this version does not serve.

        The follow-up changelog therefore has to do two things, in this order:

          1. Convert whatever

                 SELECT id FROM attachment WHERE lecture_id IS NOT NULL AND attachment_unit_id IS NULL

             still returns into attachment video units, the way 20260905235721_changelog.xml did. Until that runs, such
             a row also makes its lecture undeletable, because lecture_id is ON DELETE RESTRICT and no code clears it.
          2. Drop the foreign key FKa6apwe76xlljxtm3nimrlvppf, the index of the same name, and the column.
    -->
</databaseChangeLog>
